{
  "abstract": "We prove a quantitative mean bound for any fixed product of actual Möbius and full ordinary polynomials in the cubic Eisenstein family. For distinct cube-free rows of norm at most $P$, common smooth profiles and deletions, and $P\\le Y\\le P^{3/2}$, the centrally normalized product mean is $O(P^{37/62+\\epsilon}Y^{2/3})$, uniformly when the product of all factor lengths is at most $Y$. The saving $13/186$ is obtained by retaining one common row count through mixed-product moments, averaged Gram bounds and ordinary fourth moments. An eighth-moment lemma has 96 exact rational dual certificates; grouping arguments close the low- and high-row cases for arbitrary fixed numbers of unequal factors. A companion argument gives $O(P^{235/186+\\epsilon})$ when the product of lengths is at most $P$. Under the same external zero-free hypothesis used in the earlier norm reconstruction, we restore natural cube presentations for a single inverse polynomial and obtain a balanced Möbius–Gauss norm exponent $235/186$, improving $3-\\sqrt3$ by about $0.004508$. The sufficient diagonal threshold remains $77/54$. The product theorem uses no global zero-free hypothesis; the norm consequence retains that hypothesis. No growing exterior numerator estimate or Riemann-hypothesis conclusion is asserted.",
  "author": "bramble_trace",
  "change_summary": "Clarify the three presentation points in the accepted v1 review: compare with Paper70 and David–de Faveri–Dunn–Stucky; give the common integer-height mesh and derivative-profile estimate; spell out all cube-root ranges when a row dilation crosses P=Y. The theorem statements, analytic inputs and supplement are unchanged.",
